WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.000
Ask: how does +15 m thickness change this native shelf result?
Why: Answer a controlled question with fixed scripts, mesh and runtime controls.
Look for: Keep scripts and controls fixed; change the full synthetic thickness input.

2
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:23.000
Name a separate workspace example. Choose New example.
Why: Keep baseline and variant examples separate for comparison.
Look for: Expected: a new owned Icepack example.

3
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:35.000
Choose the two scripts: run.py and initial_fields.py.
Why: Both files validate and initialize the native model fields.
Look for: Check Choose script files (2) before importing.

4
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:49.000
Enable Replace existing script files, then Import script files.
Why: Replace prevents a starter or older entrypoint from taking precedence.
Look for: Expected: both filenames imported and saved.

5
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:57.000
Read the saved confirmation for both filenames.
Why: The saved source bytes are what the next run stages.
Look for: Set Run target to run.py.

6
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:12.000
In Datasets choose icepack-shelf-initial-plus15m.h5.
Why: Select the full +15 m field dataset rather than editing one mesh node.
Look for: Check that one data file is selected.

7
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:24.000
Choose Upload to store the selected variant.
Why: Uploading stores the variant in your workspace dataset library.
Look for: Expected: Uploaded: icepack-shelf-initial-plus15m.h5.

8
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:32.000
Select the uploaded +15 m variant.
Why: Choosing the variant keeps the accepted baseline input unchanged.
Look for: Keep the baseline file separate.

9
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:42.000
Enter the alias icepack-shelf-initial.h5.
Why: An alias lets unchanged run.py read the new dataset at its fixed path.
Look for: The fixed script reads data/icepack-shelf-initial.h5.

10
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:56.000
Choose Reference in example to attach the variant.
Why: The attachment binds this variant to the current owned example.
Look for: Bind the selected variant to this owned example.

11
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Read the reference confirmation and exact script path.
Why: The exact path proves what will be copied at the next staging step.
Look for: Expected: variant → data/icepack-shelf-initial.h5.

12
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:18.000
Open Review & Launch.
Why: The review is the final check before an explicit paid submission.
Look for: Review this Icepack example and your resource settings.

13
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:27.000
Confirm Icepack and the new Own Problem example.
Why: This prevents a previous example from being mistaken for the new plan.
Look for: The review is bound to this example before explicit submission.

14
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:36.000
Scroll through the review. All infrastructure rows must be Ready.
Why: Ready checks confirm storage, image and compute can support the run.
Look for: Keep the explicit launch action separate from review.

15
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:51.000
Choose Launch cloud run.
Why: This action stages the bound data and starts one native execution.
Look for: The reviewed variant is staged for one native execution.

16
00:02:51.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Follow the new Icepack run until Completed.
Why: The new run is the record to follow through startup and completion.
Look for: Startup and Batch task time are recorded separately.

17
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:14.000
In Results choose thickness.
Why: Selecting a field does not itself update the displayed plot.
Look for: Choose the field before rendering it.

18
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:29.000
Choose Render for thickness.
Why: Render displays the final thickness exported by the native solver.
Look for: Expected: 25 native values, about 315–515 m.

19
00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:43.000
Select velocity to inspect the native flow field.
Why: Use velocity to inspect the response to the thickness change.
Look for: The plot changes only after Render.

20
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:59.000
Choose Render for the selected velocity field.
Why: Render updates the plot to the selected native velocity field.
Look for: Expected: native speed 100–158.61 m/year.

21
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:13.000
Choose Download results. Wait for a fresh archive link.
Why: Preserve the fields and initialization evidence with runtime metadata.
Look for: Keep the mesh, fields and provenance together.

22
00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:28.000
Choose the fresh results_bundle link to save it.
Why: Both archives are needed for the baseline-versus-variant comparison.
Look for: Retain both baseline and variant archives.

23
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:40.000
Inspect initial-conditions.png from the saved run.
Why: Check that the uploaded full fields actually initialized the native mesh.
Look for: All five initial fields matched interpolation at all 25 native vertices.

24
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:55.000
Derived from saved numeric fields: compare final thickness.
Why: Shared scales show the two fields; the separate delta scale shows their difference.
Look for: Area-mean thickness: 403.64986 → 418.64758 m.

25
00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:10.000
Derived from saved numeric fields: compare final speed.
Why: Area-weighted metrics compare the full field rather than one selected node.
Look for: Area-RMS speed: 133.90846 → 137.17064 m/year.

26
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:22.000
Keep the input change and controls with your comparison.
Why: Retain the coupled input change so the scientific question stays clear.
Look for: This pair changes interior and inflow thickness together.
